[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Build Flavors, Environment Configuration, and Obfuscation in Flutter"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Production-grade Flutter applications require separation of environments (like development, staging, and production), secure handling of configuration variables, and code protection via obfuscation. This tutorial covers setting up build flavors for Android and iOS, managing environment variables, and obfuscating your release builds."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Build Flavors"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Build flavors allow you to create distinct versions of your app from a single codebase. Common flavors include dev, staging, and production. Each flavor can have its own application ID, app name, API endpoints, and launcher icons."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Configuring Android Flavors"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To configure flavors in Android, open your android/app/build.gradle file and add the flavorDimensions and productFlavors configurations inside the android block."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"android {
    ...
    flavorDimensions "default"

    productFlavors {
        dev {
            dimension "default"
            applicationIdSuffix ".dev"
            resValue "string", "app_name", "App Dev"
        }
        production {
            dimension "default"
            resValue "string", "app_name", "App"
        }
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Configuring iOS Schemes and Configurations"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"For iOS, you need to create custom build configurations and schemes in Xcode. Typically, you duplicate Debug, Release, and Profile configurations to create Debug-dev, Release-dev, Debug-production, and Release-production."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open ios/Runner.xcodeproj in Xcode."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Go to the Info tab of your project and under Configurations, duplicate existing configurations for your new flavors."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create custom Schemes pointing to these configurations."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Managing Environment Configuration"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To inject configuration variables dynamically based on the active flavor, create an Environment class and use the --dart-define flag during compilation."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"class Environment {
  static const String apiUrl = String.fromEnvironment(
    'API_URL',
    defaultValue: 'https://api.default.com',
  );

  static const String environmentName = String.fromEnvironment(
    'ENV_NAME',
    defaultValue: 'Development',
  );
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can now run your application with specific environment configurations using the following command:"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"flutter run --flavor dev --dart-define=API_URL=https://api.dev.com --dart-define=ENV_NAME=Dev"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Code Obfuscation in Flutter"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Obfuscation hides your Dart source code by renaming classes, methods, and fields to make reverse engineering difficult. To build an obfuscated release bundle, use the --obfuscate and --split-debug-info flags."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"flutter build appbundle --obfuscate --split-debug-info=/<project-dir>/debug-info/"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always save the generated debug-info symbols directory. You will need it to symbolicate any crash reports gathered from production releases."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"To symbolicate a stack trace later, run: flutter symbolize --input=<stack-trace-file> --output=<symbolicated-file> --debug-info=<path-to-saved-symbols>"},{"order":23,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"By correctly implementing build flavors, utilizing dart-define for environment injection, and applying code obfuscation, your Flutter application will be secure, maintainable, and structured correctly for multiple deployment stages."}]
